We generally require the type of locals to be well-formed whenever the local is used. This includes proving the where-bounds of the local and also requires all regions used by it to be live. The only exception to this is when implicitly dropping values when they go out of scope. This does not necessarily require the value to be live: fn main() { let x = vec![]; { let y = String::from("I am temporary"); x.push(&y); } // `x` goes out of scope here, after the reference to `y` // is invalidated. This means that while dropping `x` its type // is not well-formed as it contain regions which are not live.
} This is only sound if dropping the value does not try to access any dead region. We check this by requiring the type of the value to be drop-live. The requirements for which are computed in fn dropck_outlives. The rest of this section uses the following type definition for a type which requires its region parameter to be live: struct PrintOnDrop<'a>(&'a str);
impl<'a> Drop for PrintOnDrop<'_> { fn drop(&mut self) { println!("{}", self.0); }
}

